hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well  in his book the stoic paradox is one of  the difficult points for many people to  accept that Cicero brings up one of the  key ideas of the Stoics is that virtue  or moral goodness is sufficient for  happiness as a matter of fact you might  say he even goes beyond that saying that  unless you have virtue you will not be  happy if you are valuing other things  you will actually wind up being unhappy  sooner or later and whatever happiness  you do enjoy will not be genuine  happiness and what this comes down to in  his discussion is really a contrast  between what we can call self  sufficiency and being dependent upon  what the ancients and the medieval is  called fortune and we we might call it  being subject to contingency or events  or things that are essentially outside  of our control so why is it that there  is this this important contrast and how  does it play up this rule tells us that  virtue will in fact make us  self-sufficient why will it do that  that's a key element of this why will  being practically wise or just or  courageous or a temperate why will those  make a self-sufficient well what does it  mean to be self-sufficient it means to  not depend upon other people or things  external factors but to be able to  determine how things are for you in the  ways that matter most by yourself  through your own actions now you could  be self-sufficient in a way and also  screw up your life I suppose but the  person who is truly self-sufficient is  not going to be doing that so happiness  really cannot be taken away from them  the happiness that they do enjoy and  they will indeed be happy in Cicero's  view he's going to talk about this as  being praiseworthy what  we'll talk about in just a moment so one  thing I would like to highlight here is  mrs. in paradox - he says addressing his  audience that you don't know how great  is the strength that virtue possesses  you utter the name virtue but you don't  know what virtue itself means and if you  did fully understand what virtue is  which will entail in some some respects  not only you know sort of a cognitive  thing but a practical thing having  developed the virtues within one at  least to a certain extent or being  properly oriented along the lines of the  virtues then it's possible to understand  just how strong the virtues are how they  are able to make a person happy in in  their life he also says no one can fail  to be supremely happy who relies solely  on himself and who places all his  possessions within himself alone and so  pursuing virtue as the primary good or  the good from which other goods flow the  good that others should be sacrificed to  if need be is going to make that person  self-sufficient by placing everything  that could go wrong or could go right  within their own purview now by contrast  he talks about fortune those who place  their hopes their desires in fortune in  things outside of their control in  random events in the workings of the  world in the proverbial wheel of fortune  that raises some up and lowers others at  the same time that's actually setting  yourself up for having some some real  problems he says those who hang their  hopes entirely on fortune can have  nothing certain there's no there's no  certainty - that nothing that they can  be assured will remain with them for a  single day they may enjoy all sorts of  things in a present moment but they  can't be assured that that is going to  continue on so we see people who for  example strike it rich  and lose the money or they win the  relationship that they've been seeking  but then things don't pan out over time  and they lose that love and affection  and esteem that they had from the other  person there's all sorts of things that  can be taken away from us interestingly  in this case Cicero is going to bring up  because he's talking as if he's he's  addressing his political enemies death  and exile and by by extension loss of  possessions  now the ordinary person who is placing  his or her hopes in the realm of fortune  is going to be very threatened by death  and probably you can use it to coerce  them into doing whatever it is that you  you wish them to do so Cicero is going  to tell us how that's not an issue for  him likewise exile having to go away  from where you are accustomed to live  the place that you are familiar with and  that that whole network of relationships  that you have built up over time when we  might think about this in our own time  not only in terms of physical space or a  particular country but even in terms of  virtual space if you get kicked off of  Twitter right that's a kind of Exile  it's a sanction that people are imposing  on you loss of possessions anything that  we have that is a possession can be  taken away this chalkboard could be  taken away could be destroyed could fall  apart you know these these lights this  book this time any of these things can  go away at any moment and we can try as  much as we wish to safeguard them you  know we can hire a personal security  firm or have an alarm or you know put  our clothes in mothballs or whatever we  like you know put our bind our books and  fancy bindings you know and make sure  that it's on special paper  none of that guarantees that any of  those things won't be destroyed or be  taken away from us and turn to somebody  else so if we subject ourselves to  fortune we not only subject ourselves to  the physical  world but we also subject ourselves to  the whims and desires of other people  and there are going to be certain  consequences to this Cicero's says  people think that they're going to be  happy they're going to they're going to  be ah to us they're going to be blessed  and they're going to flourish if they  have enough of these sorts of goods of  fortune but that's not really the case  instead what they're likely to end up  feeling  besides you know momentary happiness or  momentary joy which is often excessive  is fear fear of losing these things fear  when they are threatened fear about  things that aren't even threatening them  yet but they need to think about ahead  of time and then sadness or sorrow when  these things are taken away from them or  when they are imposed on them like exile  and they will they'll also feel desire  and lust since he says to pity tasks for  for these these things they are going to  be driven they're going to be subjected  to them if you know that that somebody  really really wants the newest iPhone  you can make them do all sorts of things  matter of fact they're television shows  devoted specifically to getting people  to do all sorts of goofy things you know  motivated by their desires their fears  for goods of fortune  now coming back to this issue of being  praiseworthy this role ends paradox too  by telling us that the good and brave  and wise person now notice that that you  know wise and and brave these are  virtues right cannot be wretched or  miserable nor yet can he whose virtue  and whose character deserve praise fail  to live a life that is praiseworthy this  doesn't mean that anyone will in fact  praise them but they can praise  themselves they can wrest certain that  what they are doing is indeed the right  thing the right way to live the right  choices to make they can have a sort of  self-esteem that is genuine self-esteem  and not just everybody gets a gold  our sort of stuff and he says further a  life that's praiseworthy is not a life  to free from it would be a life to flee  from if it were indeed wretched but  whatever is praiseworthy must be deemed  to be happy and prosperous and desirable  they asked him at Florence at expending  the deity be ottoman Florence a life  that is happy and flourishing genuine  flourishing comes from the virtues and a  life devoted to virtue now I wanted to  also mention Cicero talks about death  and exile here and he says you can  threaten me and he was indeed threatened  with death but that is not going to  really affect me because my you know you  can take my life away from me you can  take my capacity to enjoy my possessions  but you won't change who I am you won't  take away as he says my glory which is  not a glory that's dependent simply on  people saying nice things about you he  also said something very interesting  about exile as well he says so you wish  to exile me exile is really only a  threat to those whose domicile is  encircled by a bounding line not to  those who deem the whole world to be a  single city this is the stoic doctrine  of cosmopolitanism of the entire world  being your political community being  your polis and Cicero is in fact  endorsing this from the stoic  perspective and saying this renders  exile less of a threat so the road to  genuine happiness does not lie in  dependency on fortune but rather in the  self-sufficiency that cultivating the  virtues affords one  you
 

